                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KLT+relations"} KLT relations
          %h1#stone_duality Stone duality
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#particular_cases"} Particular cases
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#locales_and_frames"} Locales and frames
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#topological_spaces"} Topological spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#coherent_spaces_and_distributive_lattices"} Coherent spaces and distributive lattices
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#priestley_spaces_and_distributive_lattices"} Priestley spaces and distributive lattices
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#StoneSpacesAndBooleanAlgebras"} Stone spaces and Boolean algebras
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#theorem_stone_representation"} Theorem (Stone representation)
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#StoneSpacesAndProfiniteSets"} Stone spaces and profinite sets
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#stonean_spaces_and_complete_boolean_algebras"} Stonean spaces and complete Boolean algebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#measurable_spaces_and_complete_boolean_algebras"} Measurable spaces and σ-complete Boolean algebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#profinite_algebras"} Profinite algebras
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Stone duality is a subject comprising various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities"} dualities
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            in the area of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
            and topological
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
          %h2#particular_cases Particular cases
          %h3#locales_and_frames Locales and frames
          %p
            Perhaps the most general duality falling under this heading is that between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            (on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            side) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            (on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantity"} quantity
            side). Of course, this duality is not very deep at all; the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loc"} Loc
            of locales is simply
            %em defined
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
            of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frm"} Frm
            of frames. But there are several interesting dualities between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
            of these.
          %h3#topological_spaces Topological spaces
          %p
            Stone duality is often described for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            rather than for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+spaces"} sober spaces
            and frames with enough points (which correspond to
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+locale"} topological locale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
            (or other forms of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %h3#coherent_spaces_and_distributive_lattices Coherent spaces and distributive lattices
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            generates a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
            frame. The locales which arise in this way can be characterized as the
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+locale"} coherent locale
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+locale"} topological
          %h3#priestley_spaces_and_distributive_lattices Priestley spaces and distributive lattices
          %p
            Assuming the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Priestley+spaces"} Priestley spaces
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
          %h3#StoneSpacesAndBooleanAlgebras Stone spaces and Boolean algebras
          %p
            The duality which is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marshall+Stone"} Marshall Stone
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+spaces"} Stone spaces
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            is a Boolean algebra precisely when the free frame it generates is the topology of a Stone space, and any continuous map of Stone spaces is coherent. Therefore, the category of Stone spaces is dual to the category of Boolean algebras. The Boolean algebra corresponding to a Stone space consists of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+sets"} clopen sets
          %p
            This duality may be realized via a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
            as follows. The two-element
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            may be regarded as a Boolean algebra object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CH
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta\colon \mathbf{2}^n \to \mathbf{2}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH(-, \mathbf{2}): CH^{op} \to Set
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi CH
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH(-, \mathbf{2})^n \cong CH(-, \mathbf{2}^n) \stackrel{CH(-, \theta)}{\to} CH(-, \mathbf{2})
          %p and therefore we obtain a lift
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Bool
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH(-, \mathbf{2}): CH^{op} \to Bool
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+space"} Stone space
            is by definition a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+disconnected+topological+space"} totally disconnected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Stone
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stone \hookrightarrow CH
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of Stone spaces.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_stone_representation Theorem (Stone representation)
            %p
              The representable functor restricts to an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Stone
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Bool
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stone^{op} \to Bool
          %p
            This important theorem can be exploited to give a third description of the free Boolean algebra on a set
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi CH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(X) \cong CH(2^X, \mathbf{2})
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            denotes the 2-element compact Hausdorff space, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^X
            the product space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_X 2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Bool
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Stone
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool^{op} \to Stone
          %p
            takes a Boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(B, 2)
            (
            %em this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is the two-element Boolean algebra
            = succeed "!)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi Bool
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = Bool(X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(B, 2) \cong Set(X, 2) = 2^X
          %p
            where the Zariski topology coincides with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topology"} product topology
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(X)
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH(2^X, \mathbf{2})
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subset"} clopen subset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^X
          %p
            A second description of the inverse equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Bool
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Stone
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool^{op} \to Stone
            comes about through the yoga of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambimorphic+object"} ambimorphic objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            can equally well be seen as a
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+object#comphaus"} compact Hausdorff object
            in the category of Boolean algebras. Thus, the representable functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Bool
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(-, \mathbf{2}): Bool^{op} \to Set
            lifts canonically to a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Bool
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool^{op} \to CH
          %p
            and in fact part of the Stone representation theorem is that this factors through the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Stone
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stone \hookrightarrow CH
            as the inverse equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Bool
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Stone
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool^{op} \to Stone
          %p An extension of the classical Stone duality to the category of Boolean spaces (= zero-dimensional locally compact Hausdorff spaces) and continuous maps (respectively, perfect maps) was obtained by G. D. Dimov (respectively, by H. P. Doctor) (see the references below).
          %h3#StoneSpacesAndProfiniteSets Stone spaces and profinite sets
          %p
            Note that a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+space"} Stone space
            is necessarily
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi FinStoneTop
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi FinBool
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet \simeq FinStoneTop \simeq FinBool^{op}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+finitely+presentable+category"} locally finitely presentable category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinBool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Pro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinSet
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool \simeq Ind(FinBool) \simeq Pro(FinSet)^{op}
            (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-object
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi StoneTop
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Pro
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} StoneTop \simeq Pro(FinSet)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+spaces"} Stone spaces
            are equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+sets"} profinite sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+spaces"} profinite spaces
          %p
            One way of explaining this classical Stone duality is hence via the following sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinBool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Pro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinSet
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bool \simeq Ind(FinBool) \simeq Ind(FinSet^{op}) \simeq Pro(FinSet)^{op}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"}> FinSet
            ” is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ind
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ind
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-objects"} ind-objects
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pro
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pro
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-objects"} pro-objects
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{op}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mi op
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi FinBool
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet^{op} \simeq FinBool
            is that discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "FinSet#OppositeCategory"} FinSet – Opposite category
          %h3#stonean_spaces_and_complete_boolean_algebras Stonean spaces and complete Boolean algebras
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stonean+spaces"} Stonean spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremally+disconnected"} extremally disconnected
            Hausdorff topological spaces equipped with open continuous maps as morphisms, is contravariantly equivalent to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Boolean+algebras"} complete Boolean algebras
            and continuous Boolean homomorphisms as morphisms.
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Boolean+algebra"} complete Boolean algebra
            for more information.
          %h3#measurable_spaces_and_complete_boolean_algebras Measurable spaces and σ-complete Boolean algebras
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loomis-Sikorski+duality"} Loomis-Sikorski duality
          %h3#profinite_algebras Profinite algebras
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                Stone
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Stone
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg(Stone)
            with the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Fin
                  %mi T
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pro(Fin T Alg)
            of pro-(finite
            = succeed "-algebras)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the identity theory. It is also true in many other cases, such as:
          %ul
            %li
              the theory of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+groups"} profinite groups
            %li
              the theories of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroups"} semigroups
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            %li
              the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
              (with or without 1)
            %li
              the theories of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebras"} Heyting algebras
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            %li
              the theory of
              = succeed "-sets," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a finite monoid
            %li
              the theories of
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a finite ring
          %p
            However it is false for some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %ul
            %li
              the theory of
              = succeed "-sets," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %li
              the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B3nsson-Tarski+algebras"} Jónsson-Tarski algebras
            %li
              the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattices"} lattices
          %p All of these can be found in chapter VI of Johnstone’s book cited below.
          %p
            The corresponding fact is also notably false for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Gpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Stone
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd(Stone)
            is not equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pro
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi FinGpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pro(FinGpd)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+extension"} canonical extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax+-+semantics+duality"} syntax - semantics duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+Stone+duality"} abstract Stone duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+Stone+duality"} synthetic Stone duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+gamut"} Stone gamut
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivia+Caramello"}> Olivia Caramello
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A topos-theoretic approach to Stone-type dualities
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1103.3493"} arxiv/1103.3493
                158 pp.
            %li
              %p G. D. Dimov, Some generalizations of the Stone Duality Theorem, Publ. Math. Debrecen 80/3-4 (2012), 255–293.
            %li
              %p
                G. Dimov, E. Ivanova-Dimova,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W.+Tholen"} W. Tholen
                %em Categorical extension of dualities: From Stone to de Vries and beyond, I.
                Appl. Categ. Struct. 30, 287–329 (2022)
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10485-021-09658-6"} doi
            %li
              %p
                H. P. Doctor,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The categories of Boolean lattices, Boolean rings and Boolean spaces
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4153/CMB-1964-022-6"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                , section A.1.1 of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
            %li#CCGM24
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Cherubini"}> Felix Cherubini
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freek+Geerligs"} Freek Geerligs
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hugo+Moeneclaey"} Hugo Moeneclaey
                %em A Foundation for Synthetic Stone Duality
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03203"}> arXiv:2412.03203
                )
          %p
            There is a version in model theory,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Makkai+duality"} Makkai duality
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Makkai"}> M. Makkai
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stone duality for first-order logic
              %strong 65
              (1987) no. 2, 97–170,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(87)90020-X"} doi
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=900266"} MR89h:03067
              = succeed "," do
                %em Duality and definability in first order logic
              %strong 105
              (1993), no. 503
          %p Other variants are in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Henrik Forssell,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em First-order logical duality
                %a{:href => "http://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/students/forssell.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Spencer Breiner,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Scheme representation for first-order logic
                %a{:href => "https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/students/breiner.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} E-∞ geometry
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section A of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"} Proper Morphisms, Completions, and the Grothendieck Existence Theorem
          %p
            Discussion of an
            = succeed "-version" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 1)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 3.5.3 of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
